N2  - Introduction: Gestational Weight Gain (GWG) plays a crucial role in the health of both the mother and the developing infant. Mothers exceeding or gaining below the optimal pregnancy weight gain ranges can cause short- and long-term health complications. Optimizing GWG is becoming a public health strategy that needs to be addressed for the health promotion of both the mother and infant. Maternal lifestyle factors before and during pregnancy were found to have a great influence on the management of GWG. This study aims to investigate the potential maternal lifestyle factors associated with inadequate GWG, with a focus on dietary habits, including Mediterranean Diet (MD) adherence and consumption of specific food items. Methods: This cross-sectional analysis used baseline data from the ongoing LIMIT prospective cohort study1. One hundred and thirty-one pregnant women were enrolled during the pre-hospital care before birth at Fondazione IRCCS Policlinico San Matteo (Pavia), according to the inclusion/exclusion criteria. Sociodemographic characteristics, anthropometric data (height; pre-pregnancy weight and BMI; gestational weight gain, GWG), MD adherence, physical activity (PA) levels and smoking habits during pregnancy were retrospectively collected. The maternal MD adherence and PA level were investigated using validated questionnaires adapted for the target group (MEDI-LITE score questionnaire2; Turconi et al. 2003 questionnaire3, respectively). Participants were categorized into adequate (AGWG), excessive (EGWG), and low (LGWG) gestational weight gain groups, according to the IOM guidelines. Results: A total of 109 pregnant women (aged 27-38) were eligible. Nearly half (48.6%) of the participants fell into the category of inadequate GWG (31.2% had LGWG; 17.43% had EGWG). The bivariate analysis showed a significant association between inadequate GWG and educational level (p=0.012); smoking habits (p=0.039); pre-pregnancy BMI (p<0.001); PA level score (p=0.004), as well as the daily consumption of vegetables (p=0.001), and milk/milk products (p=0.004). No association was found between inadequate GWG and MD adherence (p=0.052). The multinomial regression analysis confirmed a significant association between higher educational level (RRR=0.125, p=0.052) and daily consumption of 1-1.5 portions of milk/milk products (RRR=0.049, p=0.025) with lower risk of EGWG, compared to the reference group of AGWG. On the other hand, higher pre-pregnancy BMI was found to be associated with a higher risk of EGWG (RRR=1.358, p=0.0003), compared to the reference group of AGWG. No significant association of any of the studied variables was detected in terms of LGWG, compared to AGWG. Conclusion: These preliminary findings showed an alarming increase in inadequate GWG calling for the need to promote adequate lifestyle changes, before and during pregnancy, as a primary goal to improve the health outcomes for both the expecting mother and the infant.
