N2  - Currently, the evidence to formulate general dietetic recommendations for women planning a pregnancy is far from complete. However, observed associations between certain nutrients and female fertility can be used to guide nutrition-related decisions that should be implemented in patient care. Mediterranean dietary patterns are possibly related to an increased fertility. Women that plan to become pregnant are recommended to take supplemental folic acid. Iodine supplementation should be considered depending on the alimentary intake. Critical nutrients should be individually assessed to ensure a sufficient intake and observed deficiencies should be targeted with appropriate measures. A moderate intake of certain foods (e.g. meat, milk and dairy products, soy or coffee) seems to not have an impact on female fertility. It may be appropriate for a clinical dietitian to actively take part in patient care in order to develop sustainable lifestyle changes that consider possible underlying diseases and dietary preferences.
