N2  - Rationale: Modifiable factors including nutrition, weight gain and smoking may affect pregnancy outcomes. The importance of nutritional exposures during the preconception and early pregnancy period for both maternal and fetal outcomes has been previously proven, but relevant data are not available for Greek pregnant women. Methods: This was a prospective study of pregnant women from routine care, recruited at the first trimester scan (11+0-13+6 gestational weeks). A validated Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ)1 was applied providing information regarding nutrition habits prior to conception (last 6 months). A complete obstetrical and personal history was obtained. A descriptive analysis was conducted using IBM SPSS Statistics 25. Results: Overall, 1,030 pregnant women (mean age 32.4 ±6.7 years) were enrolled. The median BMI before pregnancy and at the first trimester was 22.9 (IQR:5.4) and 23.7 (IQR:5.6), respectively. A 20.6% (n=212) of the participants were overweight and 12.4% (n=128) obese respectively, before pregnancy. The median (IQR) daily energy and water consumption were 1,530 kcal (583) and 1,949 ml (889) respectively. The median (IQR) macronutrient consumption was estimated as: protein 53.9 g (20.5), fat 75.5 g (34.1), carbohydrates 155 g (73), dietary fiber 14.2 g (6.8). The median (IQR) intake for important micronutrients for pregnancy was 169.6 mcg (77) total folate acid, 687 mg (423) calcium, 7.8 mg (2.7) iron. Comparing these data with dietary allowances and adequate intakes recommended for pregnant women by the National Institute of Health of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, intake levels were found inadequate for most macronutrients and the important micronutrients in our population. Further analysis, with respect to BMI, showed no corelation for any nutrient, whereas there was a higher water consumption for overweight (p=0.002) and obese participants (p<0.001) compared to normal weight patients and between obese and underweight participants (p=0.001). Smoking during pregnancy was reported in 10% (n=106) of the sample and these women were found to consume higher amounts of energy (p=0.003), especially fat (p=0.016), and more cholesterol (p=0.022). The pregnancy was planned for 84.7% (n=872) of the participants and these consumed less energy (p=0.01), fat (p=0.02), carbohydrates (p=0.038), total folate acid (p=0.011), iron (p=0.015) and calcium (p=0.001) but more protein (p=0.024). Conclusion: This is the first Greek study providing evidence on the nutritional status of reproductive-aged women before conception. Our results may highlight the need for nutritional interventions before and during pregnancy to improve perinatal outcomes. References: Apostolopoulou A, Magriplis E, Tsekitsidi E, et al. Development and validation of a short culture-specific food frequency questionnaire for Greek pregnant women and their adherence to the Mediterranean diet. Nutrition. 2021;90:111357. doi:10.1016/j.nut.2021.111357 Disclosure of Interest: None declared
