N2  - Background Limited data exist on the cardiometabolic risk factors during pregnancy for Middle Eastern women in the United States. Given its unique genetic composition, cultural aspects, and dietary preferences, it is important to investigate this population. Methods We used the natality database 2016-2020 from the National Center for Health Statistics to evaluate prevalence of cardiometabolic risk in pregnant women who were born in Arabic-speaking countries of the World Health Organization's Eastern Mediterranean Region (EMR: Bahrain, Djibouti, Egypt, Iraq, Jordan, Kuwait, Lebanon, Libya, Morocco, Oman, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Somalia, Sudan, Syria, United Arab Emirates and Yemen) and compared with non-Hispanic white (NHW) US women. Risk factors assessed were pre-pregnancy hypertension (Pre-HTN), gestational hypertension (GEST-DM), pre-pregnancy diabetes (Pre-DM), gestational diabetes (GEST-DM), obesity and smoking. Results Over the study period, 57,287 EMR and 3,585,164 NHW women were included. Overall, EMR women had higher rates of GEST-DM (7.6% vs 5.5%), but lower rates of pre-DM (0.73% vs 0.78%), pre-HTN (0.60% vs 2.01%), GEST-HTN (4.6% vs 10.4%), obesity (13.4% vs 24%), and smoking (0.68% vs 7.5%) compared with NHW women. Among EMR women, there was a 18.6% relative increase in pre-DM, 41.5% increase in GEST-DM, 133.1% in pre-HTN, 68.8% increase in GEST-HTN, 25.9% increase in obesity, and 7.3% reduction in smoking between 2016 and 2020. Conclusion Pregnant women from EMR are at increased risk of GEST-DM compared to NHW in the US, despite having lower prevalence of obesity. There was significant increase in almost all cardiometabolic risk factors in EMR pregnant women between 2016 and 2020. Future studies should investigate mechanisms of increased GEST-DM risk and identify approaches to prevent GDM in this high-risk population.
