N2  - Introduction: It is elusive that whether that Mediterranean diet helps prevent gestational diabetes. This meta-analysis aims to study the efficacy of Mediterranean diet for gestational diabetes mellitus. Methods: We have systematically searched PubMed, EMbase, Web of science, EBSCO and Cochrane library databases, and included randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that reported the effect of Mediterranean diet on gestational diabetes. Results: We included five eligible RCTs in this meta-analysis. Compared with control intervention for pregnant women, Mediterranean diet could substantially decrease the incidence of gestational diabetes mellitus (OR = 0.64; 95% CI = 0.51 to 0.79; p < 0.0001), HOMA-IR (MD = −0.29; 95% CI = −0.50 to −0.09; p = 0.004), insulin treatment (OR = 0.39; 95% CI = 0.22 to 0.68; p = 0.0009) and gestational weight gain (MD = −0.74; 95% CI = −1.21 to −0.28; p = 0.002), but demonstrated no obvious impact on the admission of neonatal unit (OR = 0.75; 95% CI = 0.47 to 1.20; p = 0.24). Conclusions: Mediterranean diet can effectively prevent gestational diabetes mellitus.
