N2  - Background: Several works have addressed the effect of nutritional factors on ovarian reserve biomarkers, however, the role of dietary patterns (DPs) has not yet been established. Therefore, we aimed to investigate the associations between dietary patterns and antral follicle count (AFC), a marker of ovarian reserve. Methods: A total of 363 women seeking preconception evaluation and infertility care at the Massachusetts General Hospital and participating in an ongoing prospective study were analyzed. Women with polycystic ovary syndrome were excluded from the current analysis. Participants reported lifestyle and health determinants, and completed a food-frequency questionnaire (FFQ). AFC was determined via transvaginal ultrasound performed on the 3rd day of either an unstimulated menstrual cycle or of a progesterone withdrawal bleed. We calculated three a priori defined DPs adherence scores from the baseline FFQ: the Mediterranean diet (MedDiet), Fertility diet, and Profertility diet. Poisson-regression models were performed to assess the relationships between DPs and AFC adjusting for age, smoking status, education, body mass index, physical activity and total energy intake. Results: The median (interquartile range) age and AFC of the women in our study women were 35.0 (32.0-38.0) years and 13 (8 -19) follicles, respectively. Higher adherence to the three DPs were unrelated to AFC. The multivariable adjusted AFC mean (95% confidence interval) for women in the highest and lowest quartile of adherence score was 13.9 (13.0-14.9) and 13.5 (12.6-14.4) for the MedDiet, 14.0 (13.2-14.9) and 13.5 (12.7-14.3) for the Fertility diet, and 12.5 (11.6-13.5) and 13.3 (12.5-14.2) for the Profertility diet. Conclusions: DPs were unrelated to AFC among a cohort of women presenting at a fertility center. Due to the scarcity and heterogeneity of the current evidence it is important to evaluate this association in further studies, particularly among women from the general population.
