N2  - Background: Recent evidence suggests adherence to a fish-centric Mediterranean diet during pregnancy is beneficial for both child brain development and maternal weight management. Yet fish consumption during pregnancy is often avoided because of contaminant concerns. Women receive trusted dietary information from obstetrics/gynecology clinicians (OBs) during pregnancy, but little is known about the how OBs talk about weight management, nutrition or fish consumption. This analysis describes how OBs in Minnesota talk about these topics and identifies opportunities for future intervention. Methods: A sample of 200 OBs were surveyed via a sequential mixed-mode design. Questions asked how often clinicians discussed various diet-related topics and barriers to these discussions. Descriptive and chi-square statistics were used to report prevalence and differences across clinician characteristics. Results: Fifty-three percent of OBs responded. Those with fewer years practicing discussed weight management more than those with more (p < .01). The same pattern did not exist for talking about benefits of eating fish. Of OBs who talked about diet, weight management was a more prevalent topic (81%) than the health benefits of eating fish (31%). Almost all OBs (99%) thought it was important to talk with pregnant women about fish intake, but 76% thought it was important to talk specifically about risks. Time was the most prevalent barrier to discussing fish during clinic visits (97%). Conclusions: Results show OBs, particularly those with fewer years practicing, feel weight management is more salient for pregnant patients than increasing safe fish consumption and the two topics may be competing for valuable visit time. Pairing this with the known benefits of eating certain fish during pregnancy for both weight management and child development, there is a unique opportunity to design strategies to support OBs in discussing fish consumption as a comprehensive part of weight management rather than a competing interest.
