N2  - Introduction: In recent years, a number of observational studies have indicated that variations in preconceptional diet may impact early embryo development. Particular interest has been afforded to the possible effects of a Mediterranean diet. Important components are considered to include olive oil, omega-3 fatty acids (FAs) from seafood and vitamin D. Methods: The PREconception dietary suPplements in Assisted REproduction (PREPARE) trial was a prospective, double-blind randomised controlled trial. 111 couples scheduled to undergo IVF treatment were recruited. Couples were randomised to receive either the 6-week study intervention of olive oil for cooking, an olive-oil-based spread, a daily supplement drink enriched with vitamin D (10 lg daily), and the marine omega-3 fatty acids DHA (1.2 g daily) and EPA (0.8 g daily); or to a control. The primary endpoint for the study was the time taken for completion of the second cell cycle after fertilization (CC2). Secondary endpoints included time to complete the third and fourth cell cycles (CC3 and CC4), the synchrony of the second and third cell cycles (S2 and S3), and the day-3 and day-5 known implantation data scores (KIDScores). Results: Couples randomised to the study group demonstrated significant increases in red blood cell EPA and DHA, and in blood serum vitamin D (all P < 0.001). There was no difference in CC2 between the two groups (P = 0.707). However, compared with the control group, the CC4 was accelerated in the study group (P < 0.001), and a significantly shortened S3 (P = 0.031) as well as an increase in KIDSCore on day 3 (P = 0.05) were observed. Conclusion: This study demonstrates that a short period of dietary supplementation alters the rate of embryo cleavage. Further research is required to investigate the mechanisms which regulate this effect, and whether the impact on embryo development translates into improved clinical outcomes.
