N2  - Background and Aims: Studies have reported lower risk of type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) among individuals consuming a Mediterranean diet (MED), but the mediating mechanisms are unclear. Methods: We investigated the association of MED intake with incident T2DM among 25,317 apparently healthy women and estimated the proportion of reduced risk of T2DM explained by a panel of biomarkers (N=40) including lipids, lipoproteins, apolipoproteins, inflammation, insulin resistance, hemoglobin A1c, branched-chain amino acids, small molecule metabolites, and clinical factors. MED intake was computed from self-reported dietary intake. The percentage mediation effect was calculated: (HR basic model –HR adjusted model)/ (HR basic model -1)*100. Basic model included age, energy intake, smoking, menopausal status, postmenopausal hormone use, and activity. Results: T2DM events were ascertained through follow-up questionnaires and medical records (N=2307 T2DM; mean follow-up 19.8 years). Higher MED intake (score ≥6 vs ≤3) was significantly associated with 30% lower T2DM risk (hazard ratio 0.70, 95% CI 0.62-0.79). Biomarkers of insulin resistance made the largest contribution to lower risk (accounting for 81.5% of the MED-T2DM association), followed by BMI (55.5%), HDL measures (53.0%), inflammation (52.5%), with lesser contributions from branched-chain amino acids (34.5%), VLDL measures (32.0%), LDL measures (31.0%), blood pressure (29.0%), and apolipoproteins (23.5%), and minimal contribution (≤2%) from other metabolites and hemoglobin A1c (Figure). Conclusions: In this study, higher MED-intake was associated with 30% relative risk reduction in T2DM over 20 years, mediated mostly through biomarkers of insulin resistance, followed by BMI, HDL measures, and inflammation with lesser contributions from other pathways, including hemoglobin A1c. [Figure presented]
