N2  - Background: Ambient fine particulate matter (PM2.5) is a novel environmental risk factor for brain aging. Although the role of nutrition in the prevention of cognitive decline is supported by human and animal studies, it is unknown whether adherence to a specific neurotrophic dietary pattern might slow brain aging by modifying the PM2.5 neurotoxicity. Methods: Community-dwelling women (N=1403; aged 65-80), free of dementia when enrolled in the Women’s Health Initiative Memory Study (WHIMS) in 1996-8, received structural brain MRI in 2005-6. Adherence to the Mediterranean–DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay (MIND) diet at baseline was scored by the food frequency questionnaire-estimated intakes of 10 healthy (green leafy vegetables; other vegetables; berries; nuts; beans; whole grains; olive oil; non-fried fish; non-fried poultry; wine) and 5 unhealthy food groups (butter or stick margarine; cheese; red meats; fast fried foods; pastries and sweets); higher scores indicate stronger adherence. Using prospectively collected residential location data, 3-year average PM2.5 exposure before MRI was estimated by spatiotemporal modeling that integrated the air monitoring data and chemical transport model. Multivariable-adjusted linear regression models were used to examine the associations of MIND scores and PM2.5 exposure with brain volumes, adjusting for intracranial volume, demographics, socioeconomic status, lifestyle factors, and clinical characteristics. Results: Normal-appearing brain volumes in the multi-modal association brain region, especially the subcortical white matter (WM), were larger in participants with higher MIND scores. For each 0.5-point-increment in MIND score, the adjusted average volume was increased by 0.68 cm3 (P=0.04) in the association brain areas and 0.73 cm3 (P=0.03) in the total WM. No association with the cortical grey matter, hippocampus, basal ganglia or ventricles was observed. Participants with greater PM2.5 exposures had significantly smaller WM volumes (P<0.001). This putatively neurotoxic effect of PM2.5 was attenuated by higher adherence to MIND diet (all interaction P <0.01 for WM volumes in the total brain and multi-modal association areas), while there was no material effect modification with other volumetric measures. Conclusions: Our study findings suggest that the neurotoxic effects of PM2.5 exposure on brain aging are attenuated by aspects of the MIND diet.
