N2  - Introduction: Menopause constitutes a leading causal factor of cardiovascular disease (CVD)onset among women. Dietary patterns effects on modifying cardiovascular risk in menopause have not been adequately investigated. Objective: To investigate possible associations between dietary patterns and indices of vascular structure and function among healthy postmenopausal women. Material and methods: A cross-sectional evaluation of 310 healthy postmenopausal women without clinically overt CVD or diabetes recruited consecutively from Menopause Clinic of Aretaieio Hospital of Athens was conducted over a three-year period. Demographic characteristics, medical and menstrual history and physical activity patterns were recorded. Anthropometric, laboratory and clinical assessments were also conducted. Dietary intake was assessed by a validated food frequency questionnaire. Adherence to Mediterranean diet was evaluated through a relevant score. Pulse wave velocity (PWV)was measured as an index of aortic stiffness. Intima-media thickness (IMT)of both right and left common carotid artery (CCA), carotid bulb (CB)and internal carotid artery (ICA)and combined carotid IMT (CCIMT)were also assessed by non-invasive and easily accessible methods. Results: Regarding aortic stiffness, coffee/tea consumption was found to be inversely associated with PWV (p < 0.001)in postmenopausal women. Dietary intake of refined starches was positively related to IMT of combined cca (p = 0.04). There was inverse correlation between nuts consumption and CCIMT (p = 0.02). After further adjustment for potential confounders, multivariate linear regression showed that consumption of nuts and coffee/tea were negatively associated with CCIMT (b-coefficient = −0.018, p = 0.05)and PWV (b-coefficient = −0.327, p = 0.002), respectively. However, there was positive association between consumption of potatoes and IMT of combined cca (b-coefficient = 0.106, p < 0.001). Conclusions: More frequent consumption of coffee/tea, nuts and less frequent consumption of potatoes and refined starches can lead to better vascular structure and function.
