N2  - Introduction: Higher Mediterranean diet (MED) intake has been associated with lower risk of cardiovascular disease (CVD) but limited data is available about the underlying molecular mechanisms of this inverse disease association in human populations. We aimed to better characterize and quantify the relative contribution of a panel of traditional and novel factors to the MED-related risk reduction in CVD events. Hypothesis: To better characterize and quantify the relative contribution of a panel of traditional and novel factors to the MED-related risk reduction in CVD events. Methods: Initially healthy 26,298 women from the Women's Health Study who were prospectively followed up upto 12 years (mean ± SD: 6.98 ± 3.1). At baseline, a series of biological factors (N=38) including biomarkers of lipids, lipoproteins, apolipoproteins, inflammation, glucose metabolism/insulin resistance, branched-chain amino acids (BCAA) and small molecule metabolites, as well as clinical variables (e.g. hypertension, BMI) were measured. A total of 1045 individuals experienced a first CVD event, including 690 coronary events and 344 strokes, collected through established medical records. Results: We estimated the proportion of reduced CVD risk explained by conventionally measured and the novel biological factors. The risk of CVD decreased with higher MED intake (Ptrend ≤0.0001): Compared with lower MED intake (score ≤3), 22% and 29% CVD risk reductions were observed for the middle and upper intake groups, respectively. For MED-CVD risk reduction, biomarkers of inflammation (27.3%) and BMI (26.1%) made the largest contributions, followed by glucose metabolism/insulin resistance (25.5%), traditional lipids (23.6%), HDL (21.7%), VLDL (19.3%), hypertension (13.7%), BCAA (13.0%), LDL measures (11.2%), small molecule metabolites (6.2%) and apolipoproteins (5.6%) (Figure). Conclusions: These data suggest that MED intake beneficial effects on reducing CVD events are partially explained by known factors, in particular involving inflammation, BMI, glucose metabolism/insulin resistance and lipids.
