N2  - Objectives: A quarter of women enter pregnancy with obesity, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia. We evaluated the effectiveness of a simple, targeted, Mediterranean-style based diet with nuts and extra virgin olive oil to improve maternal and offspring outcomes in women with metabolic risk factors. Method: A multicentre, randomised trial nested within a cohort study in 5 UK hospitals between 2014 and 2016. Pregnant women with metabolic risk factors (BMI ≥30 Kg/m2, triglycerides ≥1 7 mmol/L, and/or chronic hypertension) were randomised. The intervention was based on Mediterranean-style diet (supplemented with mixed nuts and extra virgin olive oil) with individualised dietary advice at 18, 20 and 28 weeks' gestation (intervention), compared to routine antenatal care. The composite maternal (gestational diabetes or pre-eclampsia) and offspring (stillbirth, small for gestational age and admission to neonatal care unit). Results: We randomised 1252 women with metabolic risk factors to the intervention (n=627) or to the control group (n=625). A third of the randomised women were primigravida (330/1205, 27%), 60% were Black or Asian (729/1205), 69% (836/1205) were obese. The intervention lowered the odds of composite maternal (aOR 0.76, 95% CI 0.56-1.03, p=0.08), and composite offspring (aOR 0.79, 95% CI 0.58-1.08, p=0.14) outcomes, which were not statistically significant. There was a reduction in the odds of gestational diabetes by 35% (aOR 0.65, 95% CI 0.47-0.91, p=0.01), with no effect on pre-eclampsia (aOR 1.4, 95% CI 0.84-2.4, p=0.19).
