N2  - Declines in endogenous estrogen production during the menopausal transition and post-menopause have been associated with loss in bone mineral density and lean mass, increasing the risk for osteoporosis and fractures. Mediterranean diet (MD) is regarded as a healthy lifestyle factor and has been associated with lower risk for chronic non-transmissible diseases. However, few data are available about MD and body composition in the post-menopause. Therefore, the aim of this study was to investigate the association between MD score, body composition, and bone mineral density in a sample of postmenopausal women. One hundred three apparently healthy postmenopausal women from Southern Brazil (age 55.2±4.9 y, body mass index 27.2±4.6 kg/m2) were enrolled. Bone mineral density (BMD), % body fat, and appendicular lean mass index (ALMI, kg/m2) were assessed by dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry, resting metabolic rate by indirect calorimetry, and habitual physical activity by pedometer. Dietary intake was measured by a validated food frequency vegetables and legumes, fruits, cereals, fish, alcohol, olive oil, dairy products and meat, and categorized according to the median score of our sample. Women with higher adherence to MD had more years at school [11 (5 - 12.7) vs. 6 (5 - 9) years; P=0.022]. Higher MD score dietary intake was positively associated with ALMI (6.6±0.8 kg/m2 vs. 6.3±0.7 kg/m2; P=0.039) and lumbar spine BMD (1.076±0.149 vs. 0.997±0.143 g/cm2; P=0.007) compared to lower MD score; femoral neck and total femoral BMD were similar between groups. Of the nine food categories used to determine the MD score, vegetables and meat consumption was found to be significantly related to ALMI, and olive oil was negatively associated with % body fat. Multivariate regression analysis showed an independent, positive contribution of MD score to lumbar spine BMD (mean difference 0.085 g/cm2 95% CI 0.003 - 0.140; P=0.003) and ALMI (mean difference 0.317 kg/m2 95% CI 0.002 - 0.613; P=0.037); the model was adjusted for age, BMI, previous hormone therapy, smoking behavior, and energy intake. In conclusion, the present results suggest that the intake of a Mediterranean dietary pattern may be regarded as a positive lifestyle factor in the context of non-pharmacological strategies for primary prevention of osteoporosis and fractures in the post-menopause.
