N2  - INTRODUCTION: Our previous research demonstrated that a healthy preconceptional Mediterranean diet, characterized by high intakes of vegetables and fruits, impacts in vitro fertilization (IVF) treatment outcome with or without intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI). To investigate whether the intake of these food groups influence preimplantation embryo quality, we examined associations with embryo quality. METHODS: Women undergoing IVF/ICSI treatment at the Erasmus MC, University Medical Center Rotterdam, between October 2014 and November 2016, subscribed to the mHealth platform 'Smarter pregnancy' were included in this study. At baseline food groups were identified by the program and translated to a dietary risk score (DRS) comprising intake of vegetables and fruits. A lower risk score represents healthier behaviors. Resulting embryos were cultured in a time-lapse incubator for three days. Time-lapse recordings were analyzed retrospectively to determine the exact timing of the embryonic cleavage division and morphological changes. The morphokinetic parameters were used to calculate the KID-Score that ranks embryo quality on a scale from 1 (poor) to 5 (good) based on six morphokinetic parameters: the presence of two pronuclei (2PN) and time to pronuclear fading, and time to 2-, 3-, 5-and 8-cell stage. To correct for clustering, one 2PN embryo was randomly selected per patient and logistic regression (adjusted for maternal age) was conducted to analyze associations between DRS, food groups and morphokinetic embryo quality. RESULTS: Women (n=79) were on average 32.1 years of age (SD 4.8) and had a mean BMI of 24.4 kg/m2 (SD 3.9). DRS was negatively associated with the KID-Score (OR 0.76, 95% CI 0.58-0.98, p 0.04). Results of the DRS and individual food groups are presented in the table. CONCLUSION: Here we show that women with adequate intakes of vegetables and fruits have a higher chance of developing a high-quality pre-implantation embryo assessed by morphokinetic evaluation. These results emphasize the importance of preconceptional nutritional advice to women undergoing IVF/ICSI treatment.
