N2  - Introduction We evaluated the effects of a Mediterranean-style diet in pregnancies booking with obesity, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia. Methods We undertook a randomized trial in five maternity units in UK (2014-2016) allocating mothers to a Mediterraneanstyle diet (including nuts 30 g/day and extra virgin olive oil 0.5 litre/week) with individualized dietary advice at 18, 20 and 28 weeks of gestation (intervention), or to routine antenatal care (control). We followed-up until delivery, for composite maternal (gestational diabetes or pre-eclampsia), and offspring (stillbirth, small for gestational age fetus or admission to the neonatal care unit) outcomes. In an intention-to-treat analysis, we compared groups using multivariable models including stratification variables and prognostic factors identified a priori. Results We randomized 1252 women; 593 to intervention (93.3% follow-up, 553/593) and 612 to control (95.6% follow-up, 585/ 612). Consumption of nuts (70.1% versus 22.9%; adjusted odds ratio [aOR] 6.8, 95% confidence interval [CI] 4.3-10.6) and use of extra virgin olive oil as main cooking fat source (93.2% versus 49.0%; aOR 32.2, 95% CI 16.0-64.6) was higher, and mothers gained less weight (mean 6.8 versus 8.3 kg; adjusted difference 1.2 kg, 95% CI 2.2-0.2), with intervention versus control. The intervention reduced composite maternal (22.8% versus 28.6%; aOR 0.76, 95% CI 0.56-1.03) and offspring (17.3% versus 20.9%; aOR 0.79, 95% CI 0.58-1.08) outcomes, versus control on average, but not significantly. For composite components, gestational diabetes was significantly reduced (17.6% versus 24.9%, aOR 0.65, 95% CI 0.47-0.91). Conclusion Pregnancies with metabolic risk factors benefit from a simple, individualized, Mediterranean-style diet.
