N2  - Background: GDM prevention using different approaches in women at high risk have been investigated, with various findings. The effect of an early nutritional intervention using a Mediterranean diet (MedDiet) to prevent GDM is unknown. We conducted a prospective randomized interventional study to evaluate the incidence of GDM with two different dietary models. Research Design: All consecutive normoglycemic (<92mg/dL) pregnant women 8-12 gestational weeks were assigned during 2015 to Intervention Group (IG n=500), MedDiet supplemented weekly with 1L of extra virgin olive oil (EVOO) and pistachios (150 g); or Control Group (CG n=500), standard diet (limitation of EVOO and nuts). Women were followed-up until delivery. Primary Outcome: Effect of the intervention on GDM incidence. Gestational weight gain, pregnancy-induced hypertension, emergency caesarean (E-CS) and preterm delivery, small and large for gestational age (SGA and LGA) and admissions NICU were assessed. Results: A total of 874 women completed the study. CG 440, 32.73 + 5.27 y/o, 23.3 + 4.0 BMI; IG 434, 33.03 + 5.01 y/o, 22.9 + 3.5 BMI. According to questionnaires and biomarker women in the IG had a good adherence. 177 women were diagnosed with GDM, 103 (23%) in CG and 74 (17.1%) in IG, p<0.012. The crude relative risk (RR) was 0.67 (95% CI 0.48-0.94; p< 0.020) and persisted when adjusted for race and parity; BMI; prior GDM, family history and smoking 0.71 (95% CI: 0.50-0.99; p<0.048). IG was also significantly protective against insulin-treated GDM (0.41; 95% CI:0.22-0.77; p<0.005), Prematurity (0.34; 95% CI:0.12-0.93; p<0.037), E-CS (0.27; 95% CI:0.13-0.59; p<0.001), and SGA (0.21; 95% CI:0.08-0.57; p<0.002) and LGA ((0.25; 95% CI:0.08-0.76; p<0.015). Conclusions: An early nutritional intervention, using a MedDiet reduces incidence of GDM and improves maternal and neonatal outcomes. All pregnant women could benefit from these guidelines, which should be applied at the beginning of pregnancy.
