N2  - Rationale: Emerging evidence suggests that nutrition plays an important role for semen quality, a key indicator in male infertility. The aim of this study was to examine potential associations between adherence to the Mediterranean dietary pattern (MedDiet) and conventional semen quality parameters in men of infertile couples attending a fertility clinic. Methods: Data from 183 men [mean age: 38.0 ± 4.5 y, mean BMI: 26.6 ± 3.5 kg/m2] with complete dietary and semen quality data were analyzed. Exclusion criteria included pathological conditions of male genital tract. Semen quality was evaluated according to WHO 2010 guidelines. Adherence to the Med Diet was assessed through the Med Diet Score (range: 0-55; higher scores indicate better adherence to Med Diet). Multiple logistic regression analysis was used to evaluate associations between tertiles of Med Diet Score and the likelihood of having abnormal semen parameters, after adjusting for potential confounders. Results: Compared to men in the lowest tertile of the Med Diet Score (≤30), men in the highest tertile (≥36) had statistically significant higher median sperm concentration (40.0 vs 12.0 × 106/mL) and total sperm count (80.0 vs 30.0 × 106/mL), and higher total (47.5% vs 25.0%) and progressive sperm motility (25.0% vs 10.0%) (all p-values <0.05). No differences were observed across tertiles of Med Diet Score for sperm morphology and volume. Men with higher Med Diet Scores had lower likelihood of having abnormal sperm concentration [OR for tertile 3 vs tertile 1: 0.26; 95%CI: 0.10-0.67], sperm count (OR: 0.43; 95%CI: 0.18-1.00) and total sperm motility (OR: 0.29; 95%CI: 0.12-0.70), after adjusting for age, BMI, physical activity, smoking and anxiety levels. Conclusion: Greater adherence to the Med Diet appears to have a positive impact on semen quality; however, it is not known whether this translates into differences in male fertility.
