N2  - Background: Mediterranean Diet (MedD) is considered a very healthy diet useful in the prevention of cardiovascular disease (CVD). Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) is still under-diagnosed and under-treated throughout Europe. Underdiagnosis is largely attributable to the fact that up to 50% of PAD patients can be asymptomatic. The ankle-brachial index (ABI) is a symptom-independent tool that can be used reliably to evaluate PAD. The present study aims to evaluate adherence to Mediterranean Diet (MedD) and intake of antioxidants from food in unselected premenopausal women (pts) and its relation with ABI index. Methods: A group of 450 pts (age range 45-54 years) was investigated. Pts were refereed to our clinic for prevention of CVD and were enrolled only if they were asymptomatic. Nutritional parameters were assessed by a self-administered food frequency validated questionnaire (116 items) completed by an interviewer administered 24 h diet recall. We have previously constructed a MedD adherence score based on a prior scoring system developed for Greek population. Consumption of cereals, vegetables, legumes, fruit, fish, dairy products, number of cup of espresso coffee, chocolate snacks and soda drink, wine and olive oil was investigated. They all underwent ABI measurement. Results: The mean MedD Score was 32.2±6.1 (median score was 29.8; Q1-Q3 range 26-37), comparing with data from Italian population (46±8.3) was significantly lower. Antioxidants intake was higher in patients with a greater adherence to Med D and was mainly related to fruit (berries), vegetables (tomato) and in a lower percentage from wine. Pts were categorized in quartile according to MED D Score and we evaluate the distribution of ABI index within quartile. 31.4% of women in Q1 (lower adherence to MedD) had an ABI lower than 0.9 compared to 18.3% of women in Q4 (higher adherence to MedD): p<0.01. Obesity was also more frequent in Q1 compared to Q4 and in women with lower ABI. Conclusions: We found a low adherence to MedD in women in pre-menopausal age compared to general population. Moreover women with a low MedD Score were more obese and showed instrumental sign of preclinical PAD. MedD with high carbohydrates levels and rich in antioxidants from fruit, vegetables and nuts influenced the development of atherosclerosis and was associated with a lower incidence of asymptomatic atherosclerosis.
