N2  - Introduction: The health benefits of consuming a Mediterranean diet (MD) have been demonstrated by numerous epidemiological studies, and more recently by primary and secondary prevention randomised clinical trials, particularly originating from Mediterranean countries. However, the scope, efficacy, and effectiveness of interventions promoting the MD outside Mediterranean countries have not been evaluated previously. Methods: We undertook a systematic review of randomised and non-randomised dietary interventions promoting the MD among adults outside Mediterranean countries. In November 2014, three electronic databases including Ovid Medline, Embase and Scopus, were searched for trials specifically declaring the MD as the dietary pattern used in their intervention arm. Results: 2151 publications were identified, with 1273 records left after removal of duplicates. 68 publications reported 37 studies fulfilling our inclusion criteria. 35 of these studies reported using a randomised study design. These studies originated from different regions in the world including 13 which were carried out in Europe, 11 in North America, five in Asia, four in Australia-New Zealand, two in Eastern Europe, and two in Africa. Mean follow up and sample size for these studies were 7 months and 123 subjects, respectively. Most of these studies focused on middle-aged participants; the health status of participants included those with cardiovascular diseases (CVD) (n = 13), type 2 diabetes (n = 5), healthy subjects (n = 5), rheumatoid arthritis (n = 4), weight loss (n = 4) and others (n = 6). Conclusions: Dietary interventions promoting the MD among adult subjects from non-Mediterranean countries are not uncommon. The primary focus of these MD interventions has been the improvement of outcomes related to glucose and lipid metabolism. Overall these interventions were reported as acceptable and effective. Interventions promoting the MD could be of public health significance outside Mediterranean countries.
