N2  - INTRODUCTION & OBJECTIVES: Cumulative data indicate that there is a connection between dietary fatty acid composition and male urogenital organs structure and function. Hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia and oxidative stress associated with obesity augment the formation of reactive metabolic byproducts, namely advanced glycation end products (AGEs), leading to increased tissue deposition and damage. The exogenous intake and the endogenous accumulation of AGEs contribute to metabolic and reproductive abnormalities in both women and men. The exact fatty-acid composition required to induce a positive effect on metabolic profile as well as male reproductive potential remains controversial. The aim of the present study was to investigate whether switching from a Western diet rich in saturated fatty acids (SAFAs) to a diet rich in monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFAs) or to one with equal amounts of MUFAs-SAFAs favorably effects lipid profile, pathogenic (AGE levels, oxidative stress, apoptosis) and morphogenic parameters of male urogenital system in vivo. MATERIAL & METHODS: C57BL/6 mice (n=58) were allocated into 4 groups. The first group (control, n=10) was fed standard chow. The 3 remaining groups (n=14 mice/group) were fed a SAFA-rich diet for 8 weeks and were then allocated for 16 weeks to either a MUFA-rich diet, an equal in MUFAs-SAFAs-rich diet, or continued the previous SAFA-rich diet RESULTS: Elevated AGE deposition was detected in germ cells with a higher intensity in animals on the SAFA diet, compared to MUFA or MUFA-SAFA-fed animals or the control samples (p = .018). In Leydig cells, AGE localization was higher in high-fat fed animals compared to controls (p < .05). High-fat fed mice displayed enhanced apoptosis compared to controls (p < .005). Furthermore, prostatic tissue demonstrated reduction in epithelial folding, an effect which was significantly reversed after MUFA diet administration CONCLUSIONS: High fat diet for 24 weeks hastened AGE deposition in germinal epithelium and Leydig cells of mice. Moreover, it increased apoptosis of spermatic epithelium. The cytoarchitecture of mice prostate significantly changed as a result of high SAFA or MUFA- SAFA diet compared to control, while MUFA diet restored the control phenotype. Simple dietary intervention toward a Mediterranean regimen with high content of MUFA could reverse the metabolic perturbation caused by Western diet, thereby ameliorating gonadal function and fertility. Furthermore, targeting AGE-RAGE signalling pathway might prove beneficial in either treating or even preventing obesity effects before their overt manifestation without affecting hormonal milieu.
