N2  - Introduction Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (HDP), including gestational hypertension and pre-eclampsia, are common obstetric complications associated with adverse health outcomes for mother and child. It remains unclear how dietary intake can influence HDP risk. The aim of our study was to investigate associations between pre-pregnancy dietary patterns and risk of HDP in a populationbased cohort study of reproductive-aged Australian women. Methods We selected 3582 women (mean age 28 years (SD 1.5)) not pregnant at baseline in 2003 who delivered 6149 live births between four consecutive surveys from 2003 to 2012 in the Australian Longitudinal Study on Women's Health. Women with a history of hypertension were excluded. HDP were assessed using the self-reported question 'Were you diagnosed or treated for hypertension during pregnancy?', reported separately for each pregnancy. Diet was assessed using a validated 101-item food frequency questionnaire at baseline in 2003. Exploratory factor analysis was used to identify dietary patterns and factor scores were divided into quartiles indicating the degree to which the women consumed foods in each dietary pattern. Generalised estimating equations models were used to estimate the associations of pre-pregnancy dietary patterns with incidence of HDP. We adjusted for dietary (energy intake, vitamin/mineral use), reproductive (gestational diabetes mellitus, nulliparity, multiple birth), socio-demographic (area of residence, education) and lifestyle (smoking, physical activity, body mass index) factors. Results During nine years of follow-up, 305 HDP cases were reported. We identified four dietary patterns, labelled as 'Meat, high fat and sugar', 'Mediterranean-style', 'Fruit and low-fat dairy', and 'Cooked vegetables'. After adjustment for confounding factors, dietary patterns 'Meat, high fat and sugar', 'Fruit and lowfat dairy' and 'Cooked vegetables' were not associated with HDP risk. Consumption of the 'Mediterranean-style' dietary pattern (characterised by vegetables, legumes, nuts, tofu, rice, pasta, red wine, fish) was inversely associated with risk of developing HDP (quartile 1: reference; quartile 2: relative risk = 0.85 (95% CI: 0.65, 1.11); quartile 3: 0.70 (0.50, 0.97); quartile 4: 0.58 (0.42, 0.81); P-trend = 0.0006). Robust associations were confirmed in sensitivity analyses restricting the sample to nulliparous women, or to singleton pregnancies only. Conclusion This population-based study of Australian women shows an independent protective association between prepregnancy consumption of a 'Mediterranean-style' dietary pattern and HDP risk. If these findings are confirmed in other study populations, further studies are needed to prospectively examine whether this dietary pattern, implemented prior to pregnancy, has a role in the prevention of hypertensive disorders during pregnancy.
