N2  - Purpose of the study: Ingestive behavior is regulated by both homeostatic and hedonic signals. Brain imaging studies have revealed that the activation of the mesocorticolimbic neural (reward) network in response to food cues is influenced by affect and menstrual cycle phase, in that insula is activated by high caloric foods when women report 'negative affect' or are in the luteal phase. Insula activation reflects increased food appeal, and consequently caloric intake [1]. Magnesium supply has proven to be effective in reducing 'negative affect' in Premenstrual Syndrome [2]. We sought to investigate how magnesium supply might affect weight loss intervention in overweight and obese women, through the modulation of psychobehavioral factors. Methods: Fourteen middle-aged (mean age: 40.25±9.14 years old) overweight or obese (BMI from 25 to 40 kg/m2), otherwise healthy women were recruited and assigned to follow a Mediterranean hypocaloric diet [-400 kCal with respect to calculated Resting Energy Expenditure (REE)]: (a) with (n = 9), or (b) without (n = 5) magnesium citrate supply [430 mg/day, 114.6% Recommended Daily Allowance (RDA)]. Body weight was measured and psychometric evaluations were performed at baseline (t0), at 6 (t1) and 12 weeks (t2). We used the Dieter's inventory of eating temptations (DIET) for the psychological assessment [3]: the DIET consists of 30 items indicating subjective feelings of weight control competence in six domains: overeating, negative emotional eating, exercise, resisting temptations, positive social eating, food choice. DIET total scores range from 0-100, with low scores representing greater eating disorder pathology. DIET ratings and BMI at t0, t1, and t2 were compared by repeated-measures ANOVA in the magnesium group. Due to the paucity of the control group that completed the study at the moment of writing, we decided to perform a paired samples t-test between ratings at t0 and t1. Results: In the magnesium group, Mauchly's test indicated that the assumption of sphericity had not been violated for the effect of magnesium on DIET ratings (χ2 = 0.638, p = 0.727). There was a significant main effect of magnesium on DIET ratings [F(2,16) = 7.159, p = 0.006]. Specifically, contrasts revealed that ratings at t0 were significantly lower than those at t1 (p = 0.045) and t2 (p = 0.023), but no significant differences emerged in t1 versus t2 comparison. We also found a significant reduction of BMI at both the outpoints (p<0.001). In contrast, we did not obtain any significant differences in DIET scores between t0 and t1 in the control group. A significant reduction of BMI (p<0.05) was measured between t0 and t1, however body weight remained static over the 6-week period. The power of the test was, however, quite low (14.2%). Conclusions: Our results suggest that magnesium citrate supply is useful in promoting adherence to a hypocaloric diet, in terms of reduction in BMI, in overweight and obese middle-aged women. In particular, this effect seems to be mediated by a major capacity to impose a voluntary control on disordered alimentary habits in response to hedonic signals derived from highly palatable foods typical of today's diet.
