N2  - Objectives: Mediterranean diet is associated with a low morbidity rate of cardiovascular disease. Endothelial function is an important determinant of cardiovascular performance and predictors of cardiovascular risk. In the present study, we investigated the effects of supplement including olive extract and grape seed extract, two essential components of Mediterranean diet, on endothelial function. Methods: We conducted a double blind, placebo-controlled, crossover trial. Subjects were randomly divided into two groups and administrated supplement including olive extract and grape seed extract (each 100mg per day, OGE group) or placebo for 4weeks. After 3weeks washout period, they were given the opposite treatment for 4 weeks. Endothelial functionmeasured by brachial artery flow-mediated dilation (FMD) from vascular ultrasound image, high sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) and urinary 8-hydroxy-2'- deoxyguanosine (8-OHdG) were determined at baseline, 4, 7 and 11 weeks. Results: A total of 68 subjects completed the study. The %FMD was significantly increased in the OGE group (5.56 ± 0.20 to 6.08 ± 0.19%, p=0.011) although no significant changes was observed in the placebo group (5.62 ± 0.19 to 5.52 ± 0.20%, p=0.595). The changes of the hs-CRP levels were -0.20 mg/L in the OGE group and +0.06 mg/L in the placebo group, with significantly difference (p=0.001). Urinary 8-OHdG was significantly lower in the OGE group compared to in the placebo group (p=0.013). In subgroup analysis, the changes of %FMD in women or post-menopausal women were larger than that in all subjects (5.25 ± 0.25 to 6.10 ± 0.23%, p=0.001, 5.03± 0.26 to 5.95 ± 0.26%, p=0.002, respectively). Conclusion: The consumption of olive and grape seed extracts improved endothelial function via anti-oxidative and anti-inflammatory effects in healthy subjects. These findings provide an additional favorable effect of components of the Mediterranean diet.
