N2  - INTRODUCTION: Maternal nutrition is an important factor from a public health point because it is modifiable and therefore susceptible to public health interventions. In recent years, a large body of evidence has accumulated regarding the role of the mother's diet during pregnancy in different birth outcomes such as birth defects, gestational length, and fetal growth. Although few studies have examined the role of diet during pregnancy in offspring growth in Western industrialised countries, several aspects of maternal diet are hypothesised to influence fetal growth, including consumption of fruit and vegetables, fish intake, milk and vitamin D and some other vitamins and antioxidants as vitamin E. Results of these studies sometimes are conflicting and dietary patterns are starting to be studied. SUBJECTS, STUDY DESIGN & METHODS: In this prospective study over 14 months (both mothers of foetuses with FGR (cases, n=46) and mothers of appropriate-forgestational- age foetuses [AGA; controls (n=81)] were consecutively recruited among pregnant women attending the outpatient clinic at the Obstetrics Department. Foetal growth was assessed in the routine third trimester scan and confirmed at birth. Cases of FGR (foetal weight<10 percentile) included two subgroups: mothers of foetuses small-for-gestational-age (SGA, n=25; foetal weight between 3rd-9th centile and normal doppler) and mothers of foetuses with intrauterine growth retardation (IUGR; n=21; foetal weight <3rd centile and/or impaired doppler). A Food Frequency Questionnaire FFQ was administered to patients, with 127 food items to assess usual food and nutrient intake during third trimester. Results are expressed in quartiles. RESULTS: Mothers of FGR fetuses have a lower intake of vitamin D3 (p=0,048), carotens (p=0,022), retinol (p=0,017), lutein (p=0,002), proteins (p=0,046), folic acid (p=0,043), iron (p=0,016), potassium (p=0,026) and magnessium (p=0,003). In multilinear regresión consumption of some micronutrients has protective effect: vitamina D3 (OR 0.43), carotens (OR 0.44) and lutein (OR 0.28) Mothers of FGR fetuses have lower intake of vegetables (lettuce (p=0,030), green beans (p=0,030) and carrots (p=0,027)), fruit (oranges (0,014)) and higher intake of cakes (p=0,027) and pasta (p=0,044). But only orange consumption has a protective effect (OR 0.291) about macronutrients. Mothers of FGR fetuses have lower adhence to Mediterranean diet and this diet pattern has a protective effect (OR 0.18). CONCLUSION: There exist differences in diet pattern between mothers of FGR fetuses and AGA fetuses. Higher consumption of vegetables and fruit and more adherence to M Mediterranean diet should be recommend to all pregnant women but specially in cases of FGR.
