N2  - Background: Peripheral arterial disease is still under-diagnosed and under-treated throughout Europe. Under-diagnosis is largely attributable to the fact that up to 50% of PAD patients can be asymptomatic. The ankle-brachial index (ABI) is a symptom-independent tool that can be used reliably to evaluate PAD. The present study aim to evaluate adherence to Mediterranean Diet (MedD) in unselected premenopausal women (pts) and its relation with preclinical atherosclerosis. Methods: A group of 420 pts (age range 45-52years) was investigated. Pats were refereed for prevention of cardiovascular disease (CVD) and were enrolled only if they were asymptomatic and apparently free from CVD. Nutritional parameters were assessed by a self-administered food frequency validated questionnaire (116 items) completed by an interviewer-administered 24 h diet recall. We have previously constructed a MedD adherence score based on a prior scoring system developed for Greek population. Consumption of cereals, vegetables, legumes, fruit, fish, dairy products, number of cup of espresso coffee, chocolate snacks and soda drink, wine was investigated. The following parameters were also measured: carotid intima/media thickness (CIMT) and ankle-brachial index (ABI). Results: The mean MedD Score was 29.2±5.6 (median score was 27.8; Q1-Q3 range 26-33). Pts were categorized in quartile according to MED D Score and we evacuate presence of preclinical atherosclerosis on the basis of CIMT and ABI: we identified 4 groups: W++ included women with positive CIMT and ABI test, W+- included women with at least 1 positive test and W-had negative tests. W++ were more frequent in Q1 (lower adherence to MedD) compared to Q4 (higher adherence to MedD) 29.5 vs 17.1% p<0.01. On contrary women with negative test were more frequent in Q4 (44.7 vs 28.5%). Mean BMI was higher in Q1 and in women with positive tests. Conclusions: We found a low adherence to MedD in women in pre-menopausal age. Moreover women with a low MedD Score were more obese and showed instrumental sings of preclinical atherosclerosis. MedD with high carbohydrates levels and rich in fruit, vegetables and nuts influenced the development of atherosclerosis and is associated with a lower incidence of preclinical atherosclerosis.
