N2  - Objectives: The study was undertaken to evaluate the association of Vitamin D deficiency and insufficiency and diabetes in pregnancy. Materials: We analysed a cohort of 258 women of mixed ethnicity with gestational diabetes (GDM) and pre existing diabetes in pregnancy, in a teaching hospital in London for the years 2010 and 2011. All women with gestational diabetes had their vitamin D levels measured between 26-30 weeks of pregnancy or soon after the diagnosis of diabetes. Pregnant women with pre existing diabetes had vitamin D levels measured in early pregnancy (prior to 18 weeks gestation). Women taking steroids for various medical reasons were excluded. All women were seen in joint obstetric and endocrinology clinic at two week intervals. Methods: A hospital based retrospective cohort study. Results: Our population had 32% Asians, 9% Mediterranean, 32% Caucasians, 19% Afro-Caribbean, 8% of mixed ethnic women. 43% of our cohort had normal BMI, 32% had BMI 30 and over at their booking visit for antenatal care. 51.5% (n = 133) of women had Vitamin D deficiency with levels under 25 nmol/L. A further 42.6% (n = 111) had vitamin D insufficiency with values between 25 and 75 nmol/L. Only 5.4% of women had normal vitamin D levels. In the GDM group: 83.4 % of the vitamin D deficient/insufficient women required therapeutic intervention other than diet to achieve normoglycemia. 36.4% (n = 89) required Metformin plus diet to achieve normoglycemia and 47% required diet plus Metformin and Insulin (n = 42 out of 89). Only 16% of women with normal vitamin D levels required Metformin and or Insulin Post natal Glucose tolerance test (GTT) was abnormal in 11% of women with GDM complicated by Vitamin D deficiency and insufficiency compared to none with normal vitamin D levels. In the pre existing diabetes group: 60% of women (n = 12 out of 20) with type 2 diabetes on Metformin required Insulin and 20% (n = 4 out of 20) had their Metformin dosage increased. Linear regression analysis demonstrated the poorer glycemic control among women with vitamin D deficiency and insufficiency and Type 1 and 2 diabetes in pregnancy. Conclusions: We note the strong correlation between gestational diabetes and vitamin D deficiency/insufficiency and poorer glycemic controls in pre existing diabetes. Lower Vitamin D levels appear to be an independent risk factor for GDM and poor glycemic control, as observed in our mixed ethnic population and mixed range of BMI. Future randomised trials are needed to determine this effect.
