N2  - Introduction: Cardiovascular disease represents the main cause of death in industrialized countries and hypertension is one of the main modifiable cardiovascular risk factors. The Greek ATTICA study, the Greek EPIC study and the SUN study showed that adherence to the Mediterranean diet was inversely associated with arterial blood pressure. To our knowledge, there is no evidence regarding the effect of the Mediterranean food pattern on 24-hour mean blood pressure levels and, in particular, on diurnal and nocturnal blood pressure levels. Aim: We assessed the relationship between the level of adherence to the Mediterranean diet and day or night blood pressure levels in a sample of southern Italian hypertensive patients. Methods: The study enrolled 343 consecutive hypertensive patients, including 173 men and 170 women, aged 20-69 years; 140 were overweight (BMI >25.0 and <30.0) and 102 were obese (BMI >30.0). None of patients were receiving any medication (including oral contraceptives for premenopausal women and hormone replacement therapy for post-menopausal women). The 14-point food items previously validated questionnaire of the Nureta-PREDIMED investigators study was used to assess adherence to the traditional Mediterranean diet. The following were measured: bodyweight, height, BMI (weight [kg] divided by the square of height [m]), waist circumference (measured at the narrowest part of the abdomen). Ambulatory blood pressure was measured with Ultralite ABPM Monitor 90217, SpaceLabs Media Inc., Redmond, WA, USA. Heart rate was measured over 24 hours by the same instrument. The following were also determined: TSH, FT3 and FT4 levels, plasma glucose levels, plasma lipids (triglycerides, total cholesterol and HDL-cholesterol). Data were calculated as mean and standard deviation for all parameters. Comparisons between means of continuous variables with normal distribution were made by one-wayANOVA. Differences in frequencies of categorical variables between groups were assessed using the chi-square test. Pearson's correlation coefficients were also calculated. To verify the influence of more than one independent variable on the determination of ABPM parameters (day or night systolic and diastolic blood pressure levels), a multiple stepwise regression model was used. The level of statistical significance was set at 0.05. Statistical analysis was performed using the Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS version 12.0, SPSS, Chicago, IL, USA). Results: Day (dSBP) and night (nSBP) levels were negatively correlated with adherence to the Mediterranean diet (p < 0.05). When dDBP or nDBP were the dependent variable, adherence to the Mediterranean diet did not enter into the model. Conclusions: The present study shows that the Mediterranean diet is negatively and independently associated with day and night SBP, supporting the hypothesis that adoption of a Mediterranean style diet is responsible for a direct decrease of 24-hour systolic blood pressure.
