N2  - Introduction: The ankle-brachial index (ABI) is a symptomindependent tool that can be used reliably to evaluate PAD. Aim: To evaluate adherence to the Mediterranean Diet (MedD) in unselected premenopausal women (pts) and its relation to preclinical atherosclerosis. Methods: A group of 420 pts (age range 45-52 years) was investigated. Pts were refereed for prevention of cardiovascular disease (CVD) and were enrolled only if they were asymptomatic and apparently free from CVD. Nutritional parameters were assessed by a self-administered food frequency validated questionnaire (116 items) completed by an interviewer-administered 24-hour diet recall. We have previously constructed a MedD adherence score based on a prior scoring system developed for Greek populations. Consumption of cereals, vegetables, legumes, fruit, fish, dairy products, number of cups of espresso coffee, chocolate snacks, soda drink and wine was investigated. Results: The mean MedD Score was 29.2±5.6 (median score was 27.8; Q1-Q3 range 26-33). Pts were categorized in quartiles according to MedD Score and we evaluated presence of preclinical atherosclerosis on the basis of CIMT and ABI: we identified four groups: W++ included women with positive CIMT and ABI test,W+- included women with at least 1 positive test and W- had negative tests. W++ were more frequent in Q1 (lower adherence to MedD) compared with Q4 (higher adherence to MedD) 29.5% vs 17.1% p <0.01. In contrast, women with a negative test were more frequent in Q4 (44.7% vs 28.5%). Mean BMI was higher in Q1 and in women with positive tests. Conclusion: We found a low adherence to MedD in women in pre-menopausal age. Moreover, women with a low MedD score were more obese and showed instrumental sings of preclinical atherosclerosis. MedD with high carbohydrate levels and rich in fruit, vegetables and nuts influenced the development of atherosclerosis and was associated with a lower incidence of preclinical atherosclerosis.
