N2  - OBJECTIVE: To demonstrate a relationship between the season of conception and the prevalence of Isolated Spina Bifida (ISB). STUDY DESIGN: We performed a retrospective analysis of 61 consecutive pregnancies complicated by ultrasound-detected ISB in the years 1982-2011. Exclusion criteria were: recurrent pregnancies with neural tube defects, maternal insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus, maternal intake of clomiphene, and use of anticonvulsants during periconceptional period.Weused as control group a sample of 50533 pregnancies managed at our hospital in the same period. According to the month of conception, both case and control groups were divided into 4 subgroups, corresponding to the four seasons (summer, fall, winter and spring). The conception dates were determined by estimation based on the reported maternal last menstrual period. We then compared for every season the rates of conception between ISB and control group using Chi-Square test, with p < 0,05 considered significant. RESULTS: Of the 61 women with ISB, 3 met the exclusion criteria and were not included in the analysis. No woman with a fetus affected by ISB took the preconceptional supplement of folic acid. Chi-square analysis showed a significantly higher prevalence of conceptions in fall in ISB group than in control group (25/58, 43% vs 2467/50533, 25%, p = 0,002) [Table 1, Figure 1]. CONCLUSION: The study shows that seasonality affects the frequency of ISB: fetuses conceived in fall appear to develop most commonly ISB. This result may be secondary to the low levels of folates in serum during summer months, which represent pre-conceptional period, both due to UV denaturation of folic acid and to folates deficiency in summer diet. Our data support the hypothesis that a reduction in folic acid bioavailability increases the incidence of ISB even in a low risk population, as the Mediterranean one. (Table presented).
