N2  - Background: Both quality and quantity of a mother's diet during pregnancy may influence her health, as well as that of her child. This study aimed to assess food intake patterns and their influence on health outcomes. Method: Systematic review of studies, of any design, addressing eating patterns (multiple food groups) during pregnancy, reporting maternal or infant outcomes; part of NHMRC dietary guidelines (date of last search: July 2010). Results: Thirty (mostly prospective cohort) studies were included, addressing eight maternal, one foetal, and ten infant/child health outcomes. 'Prudent' diet quality (e.g. high intake of vegetables and fish during or before pregnancy) was associated with reduced rates of pre-eclampsia (two studies; 25,200 women) and gestational diabetes (two studies; 14,883 women). In a case-control study of 131 children, lower rates of spina bifida were associated with mothers consuming a Mediterranean diet during pregnancy. Lower rates of small-for gestational age infants were associated with higher quality diets during pregnancy (four studies; 48,440 women), although preterm birth (three studies; 78,412 women) did not show associations with dietary pattern. Few associations with maternal diet were seen for childhood asthma and allergy (five studies; 12,186 children). Conclusion: Encouraging healthy diets during or before pregnancy may have a number of health benefits for both mothers and their babies.
