N2  - Introduction: Changes in dietary patterns have been linked with sedentary activities, obesity and socioeconomic status. We aimed to investigate the association between dietary patterns, weight status, physical activity and parental education in school children. Methods: A community-based study was developed in 1976 children, 5-10 years-old, from Portuguese primary schools. Parental education, children's time spent sleeping and watching TV/video, frequency of sports activities, and dietary intake measured by a semi quantitative food frequency questionnaire were assessed. Body mass index (BMI) was calculated, after height and weight measurements, and categorized according to the IOTF classification. Factor analysis (with Principal component analysis and Varimax rotation) was applied to identify dietary patterns considering 10 food groups within study population. Cluster scores were then calculated considering the sum of all dietary items quantities multiplied by their correspondent factor loadings. Generalized linear models with children nested in schools were conducted to analyze whether physical activity, sociodemographic and BMI were associated with emerged dietary clusters scores, adjusting for age and energy intake. Results: Four dietary pattern clusters were identified: (1) fast-food, sugar sweetened beverages and pastry; (2) fruit and vegetables; (3) fish and meat; and (4) milk, bread and vegetable soup. TV viewing (> 2 hour/day) and male gender were significant positive predictors for cluster 1, while higher maternal education and longer sleeping duration were positive associated with healthier dietary clusters. No association was found between IOTF categories and dietary clusters. Conclusion: Higher maternal education and children sleeping duration may have a great impact on healthier dietary patterns.
