N2  - A Mediterranean diet (MD) has been associated with a reduced risk of cardiovascular disease and mortality. A possible mechanism is through a decrease in oxidative stress levels due to the diet's high levels of antioxidants; however, evidence linking the MD with lower levels of oxidative stress is sparse in men and absent in women. The objective of this study was to examine the association between adherence to the MD and F2-8-isoprostane levels in young, healthy premenopausal women. To this end, a prospective cohort of 259 healthy women aged 18-44 were followed for up to 2 menstrual cycles. Plasma free F2-8-isoprostane levels were measured up to 8 times per cycle with visits scheduled using fertility monitors. Diet was assessed up to 4 times per cycle using 24-hour recalls. The alternate Mediterranean diet score (aMED) (range 0-9) was calculated based on intake of vegetables, legumes, fruits, nuts, dairy, cereals, meat and meat products, fish, alcohol, and the ratio of monounsaturated to saturated fat. Overall, adherence to the MD was associated with lower log(F2-8-isoprostane) levels after adjusting for age, race, body mass index, serum vitamin C, estradiol, gamma vitamin E, and homocysteine (β = -0.04, 95% confidence interval (CI) -0.07, -0.02). A one-point increase in aMED score was associated with a 48% decreased risk of elevated F2-8-isoprostane levels (> 75th percentile) (95% CI, 34-60%). These findings highlight the antioxidant benefits of a MD. Furthermore, decreased oxidative stress is a plausible mechanism linking a MD to reduced cardiovascular disease risk in women.
