N2  - Introduction: Breast cancer is a frequent disease, accounting for almost one third of malignancies in women, and a leading cause of death in Western World. It is important to investigate and understand the potential predisposing factors of breast cancer in order to safely reduce its incidence. Objective: To assess the risk of breast cancer in women who have previously used prolonged oral contraception. Material and methods: This is a study of 405 patients, between 30 to 54 years-old. They were diagnosed for breast cancer, in situ or invasive (ductal or lobular) carcinoma, between January 2000 and December 2008 at the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, and reported to have used oral contraceptive pills for a variety of indications (contraception, ovarian cysts etc.) during their lifetime. The patients fulfilled a questionnaire about their age, their weight, their menarche, several diet issues and alcohol consumption, their family and medical history and the time and duration of the oral contraceptive use. Their answers were categorized into two groups [A: “<7 years total OC use” (270 women) and B: “>7 years total OC use” (135 women)] and were analyzed, using statistics (chi-square test for evaluating the null hypothesis that “the incidence of breast cancer is independent to the prolonged oral contraceptive use”). Results: The distribution of the patients, according to their age and their weight, was almost similar in the groups. Their diet and alcohol consumption did not have any significant differences, since Greek women tend to follow at large the Mediterranean dietary pattern. The mean age of menarche was ∼12,58 years-old with no statistically significant differences between groups A and B. According to their family history, 76 women (28.14%) in group A had a close relative (first or second degree- possible BRCA mutation carriers) with a positive medical history for breast cancer, while 55 (40.74%) in group B reported the above. Moreover, 25 patients of group B (18.52%) and 28 patients of group A (10.37%) reported a positive medical history of either a previous breast or another type of cancer (endometrial, ovarian, colon). Assessment and statistical analysis led us to the conclusion that there is a small (p value50.02) but significant increased risk of the disease to BRCA mutation carriers, as well as to the women with a significant medical or family history of breast, ovarian or colon cancer, who have also used oral contraceptive pills for more than seven years in their lifetime. Conclusion: The null hypothesis is rejected, since breast cancer seems to be dependent positively to the prolonged oral contraceptive use. Human genome seems to be vulnerable to exogenous hormones in oral contraceptives, but more research is needed to establish this finding in the future.
