N2  - Introduction: Cardiovascular disease is globally the leading cause of death, accounting for 16.7 million deaths annually one with rates rising rapidly in industrialising low and middle income countries. There is strong consensus that the population attributable risk for cardiovascular disease can be accounted for by nine modifiable risk factors. Global variations in cardiovascular disease risk are due to the stage at which the nation or population is poised in the epidemiological transition. Sitting somewhat uneasily with this consensus are poorly understood biogeographic phenomena such as the French Paradox, the persistently low rates of cardiovascular disease in a population with moderately high levels of animal fat consumption, serum lipids, blood pressure and smoking, and the Mediterranean diet. The Metabolic Syndrome, the constellation of major and to some extent heritable risk factors for cardiovascular disease - insulin resistance, hypertension, dyslipidemia and visceral adiposity also present challenges for this consensus. Aims: To test the hypothesis the that reproductive ecology2 and long run biological standard of living of discrete populations has been a major determinant of subsequent cardiovascular disease risk. Methods: Examination of long term trends, over four centuries, in birth seasonality and biological standard of living as measured by average adult male height in three countries with low cardiovascular disease risk. Results: Rural populations in Spain, Italy and Southern France endured severe privations in the general crisis of the 17th century, privations aggravated by absolute monarchies, mercantilist economies and inequality. Adult male height fell to 160 cm in many areas and birth seasonality was extreme with up to 60% of all children born in the harvest season. These conditions may have been, according to a new Fertility First hypothesis2, conducive to rapid natural (fertility) selection for an insulin sensitive rather than an insulin resistant phenotype.
