N2  - Background Evidence is rising that developmental adaptations due to nutritional exposures in early fetal life have permanent health consequences. During recent years favorable general health effects have been demonstrated of a diet rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, fish, and vegetable oils (i.e. Mediterranean diet). However, the effects of such a diet in early pregnancy on fetal growth are unknown. Objective To examine associations between the use of a Mediterranean diet in early pregnancy, fetal growth, and intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR). Methods The study was embedded in the Generation R Study, a prospective cohort from early pregnancy onwards. Information on maternal diet in early pregnancy was obtained from food frequency questionnaires. After identifying the Mediterranean diet with linear discriminant analysis women were classified into tertiles: 1) low; 2) medium; or 3) high Mediterranean diet score. Fetal growth measurements in mid- and late pregnancy included head and abdominal circumference, femur length, fetal weight estimated by Hadlock method, and birth weight. IUGR was defined as a gestational-age-adjusted z-score < 2.3th percentile. Results Data on 3207 Caucasian women were available. Overall, use of the Mediterranean diet in early pregnancy was positively associated with fetal growth. A medium Mediterranean diet score was associated with an increased fetal growth rate of 2.5 grams per week (95%CI 0.3-4.4) from mid-pregnancy until birth, compared to a low diet score. Similarly a high score was associated with increased fetal growth of 4.5 grams per week (95%CI 2.1-6.2). Both medium and high Mediterranean diet scores were associated with higher birth weights of respectively 47 grams (95%CI 9.1-84.7) and 70 grams (95%CI 31.2-108.4). Last, a 60% decreased risk for IUGR was observed in infants of mothers who adhered to a Mediterranean diet (medium and high score; OR 0.4, 95%CI 0.2-0.7; OR 0.4 95%CI 0.2-0.6). Conclusion The Mediterranean diet in early pregnancy is Significantly associated with fetal growth resulting in a higher birth weight and a decreased risk of IUGR.
