N2  - Olive oil has been known since the ancient times for its emollient use. Research has been conducted on the internal action of olive oil's use as well as on its external local applications. This beneficial action is ought to its ingredients that posses nutritive and biological qualities. Olive oil is rich in oleic acid. It contains palmitate and stearic acids while linolate and α-linolenic acids are found in smaller quantities. It also contains carbohydrates, chlorophyles, tocopherols, aliphatic alcohols, sterols, phenolic compounds and volatile ingredients. Olive oil's micro-ingredients include pigments, terpenic acids, flavonoides and phenolic compounds. Research has proved the numerous and significant actions of olive oil's external local application. One of these is its anti-inflammatory action that is ought to olive oil's ingredients like erythrodiol, β-sitosterol, squalene and phenolic compounds. Furthermore olive oil's use possibly contributes to the smoother development of premature born babies' microflora creating an intact corneous layer, a necessary prerequisite for their survival. Research has also proven the lessening of dermatitis levels when skin treatment with olive oil or with emollient cream containing olive oil, was applied. Olive oil also appears to protect the skin from oxidative stress due to the antioxidants it contains and also against photo-oxidative stress. Finally, skin cancer treatment constitutes one of olive oil's most important possible actions that are still under research.
