N2  - A chronic infection with an anaerobic microorganism, propionibacterium acnes, has been found in the cortex of patients with Alzheimer's disease, pointing to microangiopathy as the underlying pathology. Many findings are consistent with this hypothesis: there is indeed cerebral microangiopathy in these patients, and all the known risk factors for Alzheimer's disease beyond 65 years of age (which means more that 95 percent of the cases) are risk factors for microangiopathy: old age, apo E4, arterial hypertension, hyperinsulinemia and estrogen deficiency. A defect in the gene for the amyloid precursor protein (corresponding to one of the familial forms of Alzheimer's disease) causes in the animal model cortical amyloidosis, but no loss of neurons. - For the prevention of alzheimer's disease a 'mediterranean' diet, treatment of high blood pressure, estrogen replacement after menopause and avoidance of the metabolic syndrome with abdominal obesity and hyperinsulinemia are essential - and therefore avoidance of daily 'social' alcohol consumption. For causal treatment cephalosporines and dapsone may be tried besides symptomatic drugs and behaviour therapy.
